{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 dismisses the requested distinction as a category error for heuristic checklists, but the problem explicitly asks what evidence 'within a single evaluation' would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that 'happens to track the right answer.' That formulation presupposes that the checklist can be heuristically correlated yet not reflect genuine reliability — exactly the non-causal, surface-heuristic case SRC_001 declares moot. A concrete counterexample: a non-expert panel uses a heuristic checklist that says 'mark the diagnosis as bacterial if the patient's temperature exceeds 38.5°C.' In one evaluation the panel scores high accuracy because the sample happens to contain febrile bacterial cases; the checklist tracks the right answer via a spurious correlation, not because panelists can discriminate bacterial from viral etiology. This is a surface heuristic (explicitly non-causal), yet the distinction between genuine reliability gain and coincidental tracking is plainly meaningful — if the next evaluation shifts to afebrile bacterial cases, the panel collapses. SRC_001's assertion that 'compliance IS the mechanism' conflates following a rule with the rule being reliability-conferring, which is precisely what the problem asks one to distinguish.","counterexample":[{"checklist_rule":"mark bacterial if temperature > 38.5C","genuine_reliability":false,"panel_outcome":"high accuracy in evaluation 1 (febrile bacterial cases predominate)","reason":"rule tracks answer via spurious fever correlation; fails when bacterial cases are afebrile"}],"target":"886b2767bc45640a91b8e943e38ceaf4b3a0e2a81a71fef98c4a7536848e7522"}]}